# HTML Data Attributes (`data-*`)

## 1. What Are Data Attributes?

HTML `data-*` attributes allow developers to store extra custom information directly on HTML elements.

They are useful when you want to attach small pieces of data to an element without displaying that data on the webpage.

For example, a product card can store its product ID, or a button can store the action it should perform.

```html
<button data-user-id="101">View Profile</button>
```

Here, `data-user-id` stores the value `101` on the button.

## 2. Why Are Data Attributes Useful?

Data attributes help developers connect HTML elements with JavaScript behavior.

Common uses include:

- Storing product or user IDs.
- Keeping small configuration values on elements.
- Identifying buttons and other interactive elements.
- Passing information from HTML to JavaScript.
- Building interactive interfaces without adding extra visible content.

## 3. How to Create Data Attributes

A custom data attribute must begin with `data-`.

```html
<div
    data-product-id="25"
    data-product-name="Laptop"
    data-price="50000">
    Laptop
</div>
```

In this example, the element stores three values:

- `data-product-id` → `25`
- `data-product-name` → `Laptop`
- `data-price` → `50000`

These values are available to JavaScript.

## 4. Accessing Data Attributes With JavaScript

JavaScript provides the `dataset` property to read and update data attributes.

```html
<button id="buyButton" data-product-id="25">
    Buy Now
</button>

<script>
    const button = document.getElementById("buyButton");

    console.log(button.dataset.productId);
</script>
```

**Output:**

```text
25
```

Notice that `data-product-id` becomes `dataset.productId`.

The hyphen is removed, and the next word begins with a capital letter. This is called camelCase.

## 5. Updating a Data Attribute

You can change a data attribute using `dataset`.

```html
<button id="statusButton" data-status="pending">
    Check Status
</button>

<script>
    const button = document.getElementById("statusButton");

    button.dataset.status = "completed";

    console.log(button.dataset.status);
</script>
```

**Output:**

```text
completed
```

The element now has `data-status="completed"`.

## 6. A Practical Example: Product Information

Imagine an online store with multiple product buttons.

```html
<button data-product-id="101">Buy Phone</button>
<button data-product-id="102">Buy Laptop</button>

<script>
    const buttons = document.querySelectorAll("button");

    buttons.forEach(button => {
        button.addEventListener("click", () => {
            console.log(
                "Selected product ID:",
                button.dataset.productId
            );
        });
    });
</script>
```

When the user clicks **Buy Phone**, the console displays:

```text
Selected product ID: 101
```

When the user clicks **Buy Laptop**, it displays:

```text
Selected product ID: 102
```

The same JavaScript handles both buttons by reading the data stored on each element.

## 7. Important Things to Remember

### Data attributes store strings

Even if you write a number in HTML, JavaScript reads it as a string.

```html
<div id="item" data-price="500"></div>

<script>
    const item = document.getElementById("item");

    console.log(typeof item.dataset.price);
</script>
```

Output:

```text
string
```

Convert it when you need a number:

```javascript
const price = Number(item.dataset.price);
console.log(price + 100);
```

Output:

```text
600
```

### Data attributes are not secure storage

Data attributes are visible to users through browser developer tools. Never store passwords, secret keys, or sensitive information in them.

### Use the correct naming convention

HTML uses kebab-case:

```html
<div data-user-name="Nikitha"></div>
```

JavaScript uses camelCase:

```javascript
element.dataset.userName;
```

## 8. Data Attributes vs Regular Attributes

| Feature | Regular attribute | Data attribute |
|---|---|---|
| Example | `id="profile"` | `data-user-id="101"` |
| Main purpose | Standard HTML functionality or metadata | Custom application data |
| JavaScript access | Depends on the attribute and DOM API | Through `element.dataset` |
| Custom naming | Restricted by HTML rules | Custom names beginning with `data-` |

## 9. Common Mistakes

- Forgetting the `data-` prefix.
- Using `dataset.product-id` instead of `dataset.productId`.
- Assuming numeric values are automatically converted to numbers.
- Storing sensitive information in data attributes.
- Assuming data attributes automatically trigger JavaScript behavior. You must write the JavaScript yourself.

## 10. Key Takeaways

- Data attributes store custom information on HTML elements.
- Their names always begin with `data-`.
- JavaScript accesses them through the `dataset` property.
- Attribute names use kebab-case in HTML and camelCase in JavaScript.
- Values are strings by default.
- They are useful for interactive websites but are not secure storage.

## One-Line Summary

**HTML data attributes let developers attach custom data to elements and access it easily through JavaScript.**
